Now I see the key insight! Look at `start_routine`:

```cpp
static void* start_routine(void* arg) noexcept {
    const auto* this_ = static_cast<const RawDecompressorThread*>(arg);
    try {
        this_->parent->decompressThreaded(this_);
    } catch (RawDecoderException& err) {
        this_->parent->mRaw->setError(err.what());
    } catch (IOException& err) {
        this_->parent->mRaw->setError(err.what());
    }
    return nullptr;
}
```

Exceptions thrown inside `decompressThreaded` (and thus `decompressStrip`) are CAUGHT here! They don't propagate up to the fuzzer's catch block. Instead, they just set an error on the RawImage.

Then in `decompress()`:
```cpp
void AbstractParallelizedDecompressor::decompress() const {
    startThreading(mRaw->dim.y);
}
```

And `startThreading` calls `decompressOne` (in single-threaded mode):
```cpp
void AbstractParallelizedDecompressor::decompressOne(uint32 pieces) const {
    RawDecompressorThread t(this, 1);
    t.taskNo = 0;
    t.start = 0;
    t.end = pieces;
    RawDecompressorThread::start_routine(&t);

    std::string firstErr;
    if (mRaw->isTooManyErrors(1, &firstErr)) {
        ThrowRDE("Too many errors encountered. Giving up. First Error:\n%s", firstErr.c_str());
    }
}
```

After `start_routine` returns, it checks `isTooManyErrors`. If there's an error, it throws `ThrowRDE`. But if the error threshold isn't exceeded, it doesn't throw!

So the flow is:
1. `decompress()` calls `startThreading(mRaw->dim.y)`
2. `startThreading` calls `decompressOne(mRaw->dim.y)` (single thread)
3. `decompressOne` calls `start_routine(&t)`
4. `start_routine` calls `decompressThreaded(this_)`
5. `decompressThreaded` iterates over strips and calls `decompressStrip`
6. If `decompressStrip` throws (e.g., due to buffer overflow), the exception is caught in `start_routine`, and an error is set on `mRaw`
7. Back in `decompressOne`, `isTooManyErrors` is checked. If the error count is below the threshold, no exception is thrown
8. `decompress()` returns normally
9. `checkMemIsInitialized()` is called and finds uninitialized memory in the strips that weren't fully decompressed!

